  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Nor'easter moves away this afternoon, south of \nand parallel to the Southern New England Coast, leading to a \ndry and warmer than normal stretch of weather through Thursday.\n\nThe center of the Nor'easter was located about 100 NM S of the\neastern tip of Long Island (KMTP) moving gradually ENE.\n\nThe low is also slowly filling, leading to much lighter \nnorthwesterly breeze across Central PA and the Susq Valley with\nsome minor gusts into the low- mid teens. \n\nWidespread, thick stratocu will continue to blanket the \nCWA today with some late afternoon breaks possible in downslope\nareas across the Lower Susq and adjacent SE PA. Near 100 pct \nsky cover will persist through at least early tonight across\nmuch of the remainder of the forecast area.\n\nThe cloud cover will break slowly overnight, but more so on \nTuesday as a large sfc high builds NE from the Lower Ohio \nValley. \n\nTemps will max out in the 60s throughout the valleys today, \nthough the Laurel Highlands and highest terrain across northern \nPA will struggle to reach the upper 50s. \n\nClouds hanging around for much of the area tonight won't allow\ntemps to fall by more than 10-12F thru the night. \n\nTuesday's maxes will be 5-6F warmer than today and readings for\nWed and Thursday will be another few deg F higher. Surface dewpoints\ninch up each day, too, and will be 55-65F on Thurs as maxes get\nto 75-85F. Pretty summer-like. \n\nValley fog will form late tonight/early Tuesday AM, but should \nbe limited to patchy/areas throughout the valleys of Central and\nNorthern PA. Similar to slightly greater areal coverage of \nValley Fog is expected late Tuesday night/early Wednesday as sky\ncover improves to 50-60 percent.\n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Cold front moves in late in the week.\n\nA cold front will approach from the Upper Great Lakes, slowing a\nbit as it moves through the region. Moisture will be pretty\nlimited as it moves through the CWA Thurs PM and Fri. NBM PoPs\n(60s NW, 30s SE) may actually be a little high. But, at this\nrange, it's probably not worth trying to tweak them. It looks \nlike the front will then get hung up and stretch west to east \nover VA. At that point, a surge of moisture associated with the \nremnants of Polo will move through the OH and TN Valleys. The\nmoisture will overrun the old front and likely bring rain and\ncooler temps for the weekend.",
